                <pb facs="0833" n="739" sameAs="frus:frus1924v02/pg739"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">365.115 D 58/81: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The High Commissioner in Turkey</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bristol</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Constantinople</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-11-03T12:00:00+02:00"><hi rendition="#italic">November 3,
                                1924—noon</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received 8 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>218. Department’s 182, October 27, 6 p.m. Delegate at Angora on November 2nd made
                    representations as instructed in above-mentioned telegram and reports as
                    follows: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“November 2, 3 p.m. Discussed at length this morning with Tewfik Kiamil
                                Bey<anchor n="35" xml:id="d645-f1"/> property transfer question,
                            using arguments outlined in High Commission’s 86, October 30, 3 p.m. He
                            stated the Turkish Government considers protocol of 1874<anchor n="36"
                                xml:id="d645-f2"/> and Ottoman law of 1873 [<hi rendition="#italic"
                                >1867?</hi>]<anchor n="37" xml:id="d645-f3"/> as null and void and
                            right to acquire real property therefore non-existent until coming into
                            effect of Turkish-American treaty. I replied that my Government did not
                            accept this point of view and I urged him to examine the question in a
                            practical way with a view to avoiding an issue. He undertook to do this
                            but he holds out little if any hope of satisfactory adjustment.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bristol</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
